{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbq5BvGzCaLBnMwcbuq3", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here's a regex for ISO 8601 calendar dates in `YYYY-MM-DD` form:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n**How it works:**\n- `\\d{4}` is a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` is a month from `01` to `12`.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` is a day from `01` to `31`.\n- `^` and `$` stop it matching inside a longer string. To find dates inside text instead, replace them with `\\b`.\n\n**What it matches:**\n- `2026-10-01` matches.\n- `2026-13-01` doesn't match (there's no month 13).\n- `2026-1-1` doesn't match (month and day need two digits).\n- `2026-02-31` **does** match, even though February 31 doesn't exist.\n\nA regex can't easily check how many days each month has, or leap years. If you need that, parse the string after the regex matches, for example in Python:\n\n```python\nimport re\nfrom datetime import date\n\nISO_DATE = re.compile(r\"^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\")\n\ndef is_iso_date(s: str) -> bool:\n    if not ISO_DATE.match(s):\n        return False\n    try:\n        date.fromisoformat(s)\n        return True\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n```\n\nIf you only care about the shape and not valid ranges, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 641, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24001, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 641}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "555972db-932c-4919-849b-9fe9a0d18c05", "uuid": "dd20cfa6-9c58-44d1-9c25-ad8c437081b1", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T16:2